THE PARENT STAMP NOW FIRES ON NODES IT DOES NOT OWN

Instruct.rtn:369, the last line of opDot's groupFields branch, reads:

    if product && !product.parent   product.parent = target;

That line was written when every case in the switch above it MINTED its own
product -- a fresh `new(tag)` wrapper that belonged to nobody. Stamping a
parent onto a node you just minted is harmless and helpful.

It is no longer true that every case mints. Cases 401-405 (nexT, prioR, firsT,
lasT, nextMembeR) have returned REAL tree nodes for a long time, and they got
away with it for a structural reason rather than a designed one: a node reached
as somebody's next, prior, first or last ALWAYS already has a parent, so
`!product.parent` is false and the stamp never fires.

As of 2026-08-24 cases 2 and 3 also return real nodes -- `parenT` hands back the
actual parent and `registrY` hands back the actual registry -- and neither
inherits that structural protection.

    case 2  product is target's PARENT. Its own parent is the grandparent,
            which is null at the top of any tree. Stamp fires: the root's
            parent is set to its own grandchild. That is a cycle.

    case 3  product is a REGISTRY node. Registries are shared, long-lived and
            not obviously parented. Stamp fires: a shared registry acquires a
            parent pointing at whichever field happened to ask it a question.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY KNOWN, AND WHAT IS NOT

Known: the two cases now return real nodes; the stamp is unguarded; the fleet
did not move when they changed (52 green / 1 parked, rows byte-identical) and
`printFamilyNew.divergence` moved only for an unrelated rename.

NOT known, and this is the whole reason the file exists: whether the stamp
actually fires for either case on any live path. Fleet silence is weak evidence
and not a clearance -- a stamped parent is a SILENT mutation of shared state,
the exact shape that answers every read correctly and diverges only later, and
the fleet has no assertion pointed at it. The incantation above is a first look,
not a verdict: ROW 0 proves the accessor is live so the rows below are not
vacuous, and ROWS 1 and 2 read the same registry twice so a mutation that landed
between them has somewhere to show.

